{
    "background_story": "Saoirse was a young Irish musician who had always been fascinated by the rich history of her country. On the fourteenth day of every month, she would visit the ancient castle ruins on the outskirts of her village, where she would play her fiddle and sing traditional folk songs. According to local legend, the castle was once home to a powerful Celtic king who had been betrayed on the fourteenth day of the month, leading to a centuries-long curse. Saoirse felt a deep connection to the king's story and believed that her music could somehow lift the curse. As she played, the villagers would report strange occurrences - the wind would die down, the skies would clear, and a sense of peace would settle over the land. Saoirse's monthly visits became a beloved tradition, and many believed that her music was slowly breaking the curse, one fourteenth day at a time.",
